Small tabular health benchmarks can yield optimistic estimates when duplicate predictor profiles cross validation boundaries. We re-evaluated Random Forest--XGBoost stacking on the public Sleep Health and Lifestyle Dataset (374 records) after auditing outcome coding and duplicate structure. The source file contains 219 literal ``None'', 77 ``Insomnia'', and 78 ``Sleep Apnea'' labels, with no blank outcome values. After identifier removal and deterministic preparation, 265 rows duplicated a retained predictor profile and 22 of 109 profiles had conflicting recorded outcomes. We therefore used two repetitions of five-fold stratified group validation and generated stacking meta-features by four-fold group-aware out-of-fold fitting with preprocessing refit in each fold. No test-fold tuning was performed. Mean outer-fold accuracy (standard deviation) was 0.887 (0.034) for stacking, 0.890 (0.033) for soft voting, and 0.896 (0.032) for logistic regression; macro-F1 values were 0.832, 0.835, and 0.852, respectively. The mean stacking-minus-soft-voting accuracy difference was -0.0031 (bootstrap 95\% interval -0.0162 to 0.0070), and the first-repeat McNemar test was non-significant ($p=0.6875$). The contribution is a reproducible benchmark audit for recorded dataset labels; it is not clinical screening or diagnostic evidence.
